      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Spinal cord injury (SCI) can cause paralysis; sensory impairment; autonomic nervous system dysfunction; and bowel, bladder, and sexual dysfunction. These impairments may lead to immobility, physical dependence, and alterations in lifestyle and self-esteem. The addition of chronic, intractable pain to these impairments can be truly devastating. Chronic pain superimposed on spinal cord injury can virtually drain the individual of strength, motivation, and will. For the spinal cord injury survivor who already faces functional loss, severe pain can further restrict even the diversional activities that are available. Thus, it may become impossible for the individual to escape his or her pain even temporarily. The various medical, physical, and surgical treatments considered to be efficacious in treating this pain are reviewed. However, although chronic pain in SCI may be managed by these therapies, a permanent cure may not result."},
